WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:06.910
Next question today is, Hey, do you believe
that any state is going to flip party in the

00:00:06.910 --> 00:00:11.950
2020 presidential election from the 2016 results?

00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:15.020
There's actually a much bigger conversation
to be had here, but first let's think it through.

00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:17.750
I think Arizona is certainly a possibility.

00:00:17.750 --> 00:00:24.990
Um, Donald Trump is a, the Democrats had a
very strong showing in Arizona, um, during

00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:28.750
the midterms and polling is looking very strong
in Arizona.

00:00:28.750 --> 00:00:35.720
So Arizona is a possibility that that might
flip in 2020 when we talk about key States

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:40.850
that went to Trump, uh, by a little bit, but,
uh, in 2016 but could go the other way.

00:00:40.850 --> 00:00:44.450
North Carolina is certainly a possibility.

00:00:44.450 --> 00:00:51.510
George is a possibility, although less likely,
I think if Georgia goes blue in 2020, it will

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:53.489
be a landslide against Donald Trump.

00:00:53.489 --> 00:00:58.641
I find it hard to believe there would be a
situation where Georgia is isolated as a state

00:00:58.641 --> 00:01:04.040
that flips and otherwise the election is basically
as in 2016 even though we have 50 individual

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:07.150
elections, there are still trends and there
is still momentum.

00:01:07.150 --> 00:01:12.789
And I think if Georgia goes blue, um, a lot
of other things will go blue as well.

00:01:12.789 --> 00:01:19.829
I think it's conceivable that Minnesota, if
you want to find a blue state that could go

00:01:19.829 --> 00:01:21.590
to Trump, it's possible.

00:01:21.590 --> 00:01:27.140
Minnesota has been sort of a right leaning
a little bit lately and the numbers are not

00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:30.999
looking as cleanly positive for the eventual
democratic nominee.

00:01:30.999 --> 00:01:33.539
I don't think that it's super likely.

00:01:33.539 --> 00:01:36.759
There are people talking about New Hampshire
as well.

00:01:36.759 --> 00:01:41.380
Um, I think that there's actually another
interesting possibility if we want to.

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:43.599
So look at Florida for example.

00:01:43.599 --> 00:01:48.630
Florida is a state that is a very important
state in many elections because it sometimes

00:01:48.630 --> 00:01:53.219
goes to the Democrats, sometimes it goes to
the Republican and it's often pretty close.

00:01:53.219 --> 00:01:59.799
I think that in 2020, we could have a pretty
wacky result where the popular vote results

00:01:59.799 --> 00:02:05.859
are very similar to 2016 and that the democratic
challenger will win by several million votes.

00:02:05.859 --> 00:02:12.000
But you could either have a Trump reelection
with a similar electorial vote margin, uh,

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:17.920
as in 2016 or you could have a very similar
popular vote result and a democratic win if

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:22.680
some key States, like for example, there,
there was a map I was looking at Florida and

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:29.140
Ohio could remain with Trump, but he could
still lose Elekta morally.

00:02:29.140 --> 00:02:34.570
And um, there are some pretty wacky maps that,
uh, have been put together that are at least

00:02:34.570 --> 00:02:38.250
conceivably plausible based on state polling
right now.

00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:44.090
But if I had to say, what is the the most
likely flip, I think it's Arizona to go blue,

00:02:44.090 --> 00:02:46.530
North Carolina, maybe to go blue.

00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:49.820
Uh, the governor there is somewhat popular.

00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:55.910
Um, and I, I think the one thing to consider
when we look at these numbers is that because

00:02:55.910 --> 00:03:01.530
there is no democratic nominee yet, the Republican
propaganda machine has not yet been able to

00:03:01.530 --> 00:03:05.050
just focus on any one democratic candidate.

00:03:05.050 --> 00:03:08.950
Whereas every democratic candidate has been
talking about Donald Trump.

00:03:08.950 --> 00:03:12.030
Sometimes even during the debates, democratic
candidates won't even talk about their own

00:03:12.030 --> 00:03:13.030
policy.

00:03:13.030 --> 00:03:15.870
They will just, there'll be asked a question
about what they would do and they will just

00:03:15.870 --> 00:03:17.150
criticize Donald Trump.

00:03:17.150 --> 00:03:19.400
We've seen a Pete Buddha judge do it.

00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:20.840
We've seen CommonWell Harris do it.

00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:24.780
I don't, I won't list everybody because almost
everyone has done it at least at some point

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:25.780
during the debates.

00:03:25.780 --> 00:03:33.970
Uh, I think the most likely scenario in 2020
is very similar results nationally in the

00:03:33.970 --> 00:03:40.020
popular vote and close results in some of
these key States, which might go the other

00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:41.020
way this time.

00:03:41.020 --> 00:03:45.100
So I think that if a democratic candidate
is going to win and defeat Trump in November

00:03:45.100 --> 00:03:51.540
of 2020 you will see a similar popular vote
win as we saw in 2016 two three, 4 million

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:53.150
votes, something like that.

00:03:53.150 --> 00:03:58.650
But you will see small margins go the other
way in those few key States that that made

00:03:58.650 --> 00:04:03.760
it for Trump, it was Wisconsin, it was Michigan,
Pennsylvania, North Carolina.

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:06.640
That's where I think you will see this election
won or lost.

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:08.520
It's not a reason to ignore other places.

00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:12.819
You still got to work in other places, but
I think ultimately small margins in those

00:04:12.819 --> 00:04:14.530
key States are going to be the difference
maker.

00:04:14.530 --> 00:04:15.530
Last question today.

00:04:15.530 --> 00:04:16.530
Hey David.

00:04:16.530 --> 00:04:17.530
How will Joe Biden handle Ukraine attacks
from Donald Trump if he becomes the nominee?

00:04:17.530 --> 00:04:18.530
This, this is a good question because this
has already come up and Joe Biden is completely

00:04:18.530 --> 00:04:19.530
mangled the answer.

00:04:19.530 --> 00:04:20.530
I believe that Joe Biden needs to fight back
when that issue comes up.

00:04:20.530 --> 00:04:21.530
If Joe Biden is the nominee and issues of
Hunter Biden's job at the Ukrainian gas company

00:04:21.530 --> 00:04:22.530
are woven into the discussion about Trump
and impeachment.

00:04:22.530 --> 00:04:23.530
Other day I saw an interview that Joe Biden
gave where he was asked about it and he just,

00:04:23.530 --> 00:04:24.530
he seemed totally disoriented.

00:04:24.530 --> 00:04:25.530
He seemed dazed almost.

00:04:25.530 --> 00:04:26.530
How has he not come up with a strong answer
on this issue?

00:04:26.530 --> 00:04:27.530
He's already looking weak about it.

00:04:27.530 --> 00:04:28.530
So a few thoughts that I have.

00:04:28.530 --> 00:04:29.530
Number one, Biden needs a strategy.

00:04:29.530 --> 00:04:30.530
I would sort of workshop it and there's a
few different angles you could take.

00:04:30.530 --> 00:04:30.546
One angle you could take and say, Oh, hold
on a second, hold on a second.

